Abstract
Application of Machine Learning (ML) methods for the determination of the gas adsorption capacities of nanomaterials, such as the Metal–Organic Frameworks (MOF), has been extensively investigated over the last few years as a computational efficient alternative of time consuming and computational demanding molecular simulations. Depending on the thermodynamic conditions and the adsorbed gas, ML has been found to provide very accurate results. In this work, we go one step further and we introduce chemical intuition in our descriptors by using the “type” of the atoms in the structure, instead of the previously used building blocks, to account for the chemical character of the MOF. ML predictions for the methane and carbon dioxide adsorption capacities of several tens of thousands of hypothetical MOFs are evaluated at various thermodynamic conditions using the random forest algorithm. For all cases examined, the use of atom types instead of building blocks leads to significantly more accurate predictions, while the number of MOFs needed for the training of the ML algorithm in order to achieve a specified accuracy can be reduced by an order of magnitude. More importantly, since practically there are unlimited number of building blocks that materials can be made of but a limited number of atom types, the proposed approach is more general and can be considered as universal. The universality and transferability was proved by predicting the adsorption properties of a completely different family of materials after the training of the ML algorithm in MOFs.
## Introduction
Since their discovery, Metal–Organic Frameworks (MOFs) attracted significant scientific attention for a variety of important applications. $^{1-4}$ Together with the experimental work, there is also a substantial theoretical support in the field. Theory unquestionably plays a significant role in the development of the field, mainly in two ways: by explaining the experimental results and by leading the experiments. In the literature there are several methodologies based on ab initio and classical principles. Last year we went one step further by introducing Machine Learning (ML) techniques for predicting adsorption capacities of different gases in MOFs. 5 We developed, trained and tested different Machine Learning algorithms for predicting $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and CO $_2$ absorption in 100 already synthesized MOFs.
Machine learning techniques are powerful tools already used in science and industry. In the field of materials science, the computational cost of the machine learning methods is by several orders of magnitude lower than that of the previously mentioned conventional approaches. However, their ability to provide accurate predictions strongly depends on the determination of the appropriate parameters (a.k.a. predictors or descriptors) that will allow the algorithm to efficiently learn from existed data and situations. Other critical factors that affect the accuracy of the predictions are the size of the dataset used for the training of the algorithm (commonly called training set) as well as the accuracy of the provided information. Among the most recent and promising works, Ryan et al., 6 proposed a crystal structure ML model, the predictions of which can be potentially used for exploratory synthesis by reducing the number of the most promising compositions to be pursued. The ML model was based on a dataset of more than 50,000 carefully selected experimentally synthesized inorganic crystal structures, while as descriptors were used solely the unequivocal parameters of geometrical and topological features of the constituent atoms.
Recently $^7$ we expanded our previous study 5 by applying 4 selected ML methodologies, namely, Linear Regression, Support Vector Machine Regression, Random Forest and Boosted Regression Trees, in a virtual space of 137,953 hypothetical MOFs. 8 In this way we determined the number of MOFs that is required for a good training of our ML algorithm together with the limitations of the methods. 7 From our experience in this recently developed subfield of Artificial Intelligence, we concluded that there are three major factors playing a dominant role in the accuracy of the model: the size of the training set, the quality of the predictors and the application of the proper ML method. So far, structural features of MOFs, such as the void fraction, the mass density and the surface area, combined with their chemical character, as it is expressed from the building blocks that comprise the framework (i.e. organic linkers, metal clusters, and functional groups), have been mainly used as predictors (hereafter called structural and chemical descriptors respectively). It was found that the inclusion of the previous chemical predictors significantly improves the accuracy of the ML predictions, in comparison to the results obtained using only the structural descriptors. Although the results of the previous approach are promising it is worth recognizing that, since there are numerous building blocks that can be combined together for the construction of a MOF, their use as descriptors in the ML algorithm will result to a minimum transferability of the model and the demand of a significantly large training data base.
In this work we introduce a Universal Machine Learning algorithm based on fundamental chemical properties. The main difference from previous models is that our predictors are the “atom types” that the skeletons of the materials consist of and not their general structural characteristics. In this way we go one step down in the size of the predictors aiming for the transferability of our model. It is worth pointing out that going to the limit and choosing the atoms of the material as predictors, results to the loss of chemical character.
Atom types are very well known for decades from the development of classical interatomic potentials (a.k.a. force fields) appropriate for Molecular Dynamics and Monte Carlo simulations. Interatomic potentials are mathematical functions for calculating the potential energy of a system of atoms with given positions in space. They are widely used as the physical basis of molecular mechanics and molecular dynamics simulations in chemistry, molecular physics and materials physics. From the very beginning of their development, it was clear that if they are based on simple atoms, the results are not transferable since atoms interact differently according to their environment. The most striking example is the carbon atom which has a large variety of interactions depending on its hybridization in the chemical compound that participates (sp1, sp2, sp3 character). Thus, it was clear that in order for an interatomic potential to be transferable, it has to be based on different types of atoms i.e. atom types that include the chemical character. That was a clever and simple way to include the chemical intuition in the force field by introducing the chemical character of various molecular species. Among the first force fields developed, which are still commonly used today are the OPLS $^{9}$ and MM3. 10
In the present work, we apply ML techniques for the study of the MOFs adsorption capacities and we examine whether the use of the atom types instead of the building blocks improves the predictions of the ML algorithm. From the previous discussion it is evident that the use of atom types for the description of the chemical character of a MOF is a more general approach. It is important, therefore to examine and compare the accuracy of the predictions of the ML methods when the two different sets of chemical descriptors are used. The convergence of the two approaches with the training set size is also examined. Methane and carbon dioxide are the adsorbed gases considered in this study. For both gases, sufficient information required for the application of ML techniques exists in the literature.
## Technical details
## MOF Database
As in our previous study, 7 the database of hypothetical MOFs (hMOFs) created by Wilmer et al. 8 was used for the training and validation of the machine learning models examined in this work. Details for the construction of the database can be found in the original works. 8 Briefly, the database contains the atomic coordinates of 137,953 structures that were created by combining together the building blocks that a MOF consists of (i.e. metallic corners, organic linkers and functional groups). More specifically, 5 different metallic corners, 30 linkers and 16 functional groups were used. For these structures, grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations were performed and the methane and carbon dioxide $^{11}$ adsorption capacities of MOFs were determined for T=298 K and various pressures. For methane the pressure range is between P=1 and 248 bar and for carbon dioxide the range is between 0.05 and 2.5 bar. Notice that not all MOFs were studied by molecular simulations at the whole pressure range.
The structures of hMOFs are available on http://hmofs.northwestern.edu, while Prof. Wilmer kindly provided us the databases with the methane and carbon dioxide adsorption capacities obtained from their GCMC simulations, along with several other important physical properties of MOFs (see below) that were also used in the present study.
## Geometrical and Chemical Descriptors
Similar to our previous work, 7 a set of structural features of MOFs were used as descriptors here. The list of these descriptors is summarized in Table 1 along with their minimum, maximum and mean values. As explained below, datasets of different sizes were used by the ML algorithm at the various pressures examined. The values in Table 1 were computed using 70,608 MOFs, which correspond to the dataset for methane at P=1 bar and is the largest dataset used in the present study.
In our previous work 7 the building blocks of MOFs were used to account for the chemical character of the material. In the present one, the types of the atoms that each structure consists of is used instead. We used the Python program lammps_interface 12 (https://github.com/ peteboyd/lammps_interface), in order to identify the type of atoms in a MOF. The code is aiming to create input files for the Lammps Package 13 (http://lammps.sandia.gov) , however, we used it here only for the atom types identification. The program, after reading the atomic coordinates, is able to enumerate the bonds, angles, torsions and pair interactions in the structure and to identify the atom types based on the specified force field. In this work the UFF4MOF force field $^{14,15}$ was chosen.
Different atom types were selected in a way to represent all different elements and to account for all different hybridization and connectivity types. For H, F, Br and Cl atoms a single atom type was chosen. For the Carbon and Nitrogen atoms a variety of atom types denoted as (C_1, C_2, C_3 and C_R) and the (N_1, N_2, N_3 and N_R), respectively, were identified depending on the formation of single, double, triple, and aromatic bonds. The Oxygen atom can form double, single and aromatic bonds (denoted as O_2, O_3 and O_R respectively) while if the atom is present in the metal corner of IRMOFs, the type O_3_f is assigned. For metals, the Zn3f2 is used to describe the tetrahedrally coordinated ZnII ions and Zn4+2 and Cu4+2 for the respective Zn- and Cu- containing paddle wheel metal corners of MOFs. Finally, Zr8f4 is used for the octahedral Zr central sites.
Notice that once the connectivity between the atoms of a structure is known (e.g. bonds, angles and torsions) the appropriate atom type can be easily assigned to each atom. However, since no information about the connectivity is provided in the crystallographic information files (CIF) of hMOFs, these were determined using the lammps_interface. Unfortunately, the program was not able to identify the atom types for all structures of hMOFs. Out of the 137,953 structures that were tried, the atom types were identified only for ∼ 78,000 of them. Also, since the methane adsorption capacity was computed in a different number of hMOFs at each pressure, a different dataset was prepared for each pressure. Even though this number is almost half of the original database, it is still sufficiently large for the purpose of our investigation. In order to avoid any bias in the comparison of the different approaches, for a given pressure a MOF was included in the dataset only when all information needed from all approaches was known. In this way the results of each approach were obtained using exactly the same set of MOFs. The size of the datasets used for methane and carbon dioxide at each pressure is given in the next section.
It should be emphasized that the number of atoms and atom types in the unit cell of a MOF is an extensive quantity depending on the dimensions of the cell. Therefore, it should not be directly provided as a descriptor to the ML algorithm. By dividing the number of atom types with the volume of the unit cell it becomes an intensive quantity that corresponds to the number density. In total 20 different types of atoms were identified in all MOFs examined and were used as descriptors in the ML method. In Table 2 the minimum, maximum and mean values of the atom types number density are tabulated.
## Machine Learning Algorithms
In contrast to our recent work, 7 where the automated tool Just Add Data (JAD Bio; Gnosis Data Analysis PC; www.gnosisda.com) was used, in the present one all calculations were performed with codes developed in the Python programming language and using the broadly applicable Random Forest (RF) 16 machine learning technique as implemented in the scikit-learn module $^{17}$ of Python.
One of the goals of the present work is to provide estimations for the accuracy of the predictions of the ML algorithm, as a function of the training set size. In order to achieve that, training set sizes ranging between 50 and 10,000 samples were randomly chosen, while the remaining data of the data set were used for the validation of the model. To ensure that the obtained results are independent on a particular choice of the training set, the procedure was repeated 100 times. This step is very important, in particular for small training set sizes for which some atom types may not be included at all in one training set. As an example, Figure 1 shows the results for one of these runs for methane and carbon dioxide. The reported results for each training set size were taken as the average of the 100 runs.
For the overall performance of the algorithm, the $k$ -fold cross validation method $^{18}$ (CV) was used for $k=10$ folds. According to the $k$ -fold CV methodology, the whole dataset is randomly divided in $k$ equal groups of data. $k-1$ of them are used for the training of the algorithm and the last one for the validation of the trained model. Since there are $k$ groups of data, the procedure is repeated $k$ times, using each time a different block of data for the validation of the algorithm and the remaining $k-1$ for the training of it. The final evaluation of the model is obtained as the average of the model performance over all $k$ validation sets.
## Results and Discussion
## Methane Adsorption in MOFs
The accuracy of the ML method in the prediction of the methane adsorption capacities of MOFs is evaluated using the R-squared ( $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ ), mean absolute error (MAE) and root-mean-square error (RMSE) metrics. Note that higher values of $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ and lower values of MAE and RMSE at a given pressure, indicate better accuracy of the ML predictions. The results of the 10-fold CV method for the four different pressures examined (P=1 , 5.8 , 35 , and 65 bar) are tabulated in Table 3. The same quantities computed using training set sizes of 10,000 MOFs following the previously described procedure are also tabulated. For each pressure, the results of three different sets of descriptors are reported, namely:
the structural descriptors alone, the structural descriptors joined with the MOF building blocks, • the structural descriptors joined with the atom types number density (i.e. number of atom types in the unit cell of MOF over the unit cell volume)
In the case where the structural features alone or combined with the building blocks are used as descriptors in the ML algorithm, the results should be identical with that of our previous work. 7 The small differences observed should be attributed to the different sizes of the databases used in the two works. It should be also mentioned that for P=35 bar in the case that only structural descriptors are used, the present results are very close to those reported by Pardakhti et al. 19 . For example, for a training size of 10,000 (slightly higher in Ref $^{19}$ ) the $\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.85$ calculated in this work, is very close to the value of $\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.84$ in Ref. 19
The results of Table 3 clearly show that the use of the atom types instead of the building blocks as descriptors in the ML method provides more accurate predictions. Given that the chemical composition of a specific nanomaterial is known and the atom types can be identified, their adoption as descriptors in the ML technique is, therefore, preferable. Notice also that, for all sets of descriptors, improved ML predictions are observed as pressure increases. It is known that at the limits of low pressures/low gas loads and high pressures/high gas loads, different factors determine decisively the amount of the adsorbed gas. At high pressures the gas uptake is mainly determined from the void fraction (available free volume in the framework). 20 The void fraction was included in all sets of descriptors examined. On the other hand, at low pressures the strength of interactions between the guest gas and the framework is important. The present sets of descriptors, due to the unavoidable loss of information (e.g. the positions of the atoms in the framework are not taken into account), leads to poorer predictions at the low pressure limit.
Another factor that clearly favors the use of atom types over the building blocks is the rate of improvement in the accuracy of the ML predictions with the size of the training set. Definitely, in cases where a very large dataset similar to the one used here is available, there is enough information for the accurate training of the ML algorithm. Unfortunately, in cases where a study is intended to be based only on experimentally synthesized MOFs then the available information is rather limited. It is therefore important to examine not only the accuracy of the ML algorithm in the asymptotic limit (as it is expressed with the $k$ -fold CV approach) but also with respect to the training set size. In Figure 2, the $\mathrm{R^{2}}$ and the MAE are shown as a function of the training set size (up to 5000 MOFs) for the three different sets of descriptors examined in this work. Each row of graphs corresponds to a different pressure (P=5.8 , $^ Ḋ 35 Ḍ$ , and 65 bar). It is evident that, for all pressures, even for very small training set sizes, the accuracy of the predictions is significantly higher when the structural descriptors are combined with the atom types, compared to the two other approaches. In the case that the structural descriptors are used alone in the ML method, the accuracy of the obtained results is the lowest for any training set size, while, when combined with the MOF building blocks the accuracy is in-between the two other cases. Based on the $\mathrm{R^{2}}$ , it can also be seen, that for the same set of descriptors, the ML predictions improve as pressure increases.
