Comparable results are found in the case that MAE and RMSE are used instead of $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ . Note that similar conclusions are drawn when the same analysis for methane is performed at different pressures, namely, less data by an order of magnitude or by 3-6 times are needed in order the accuracy of the structural descriptors with atom types to reach that of the structural descriptors alone or combined with the building blocks, respectively.
## Carbon Dioxide Adsorption in MOFs
For the case of carbon dioxide similar conclusions with methane were drawn. In this case, it was examined the CO $^2$ adsorption capacities of MOFs at pressures, 0.05 , 0.5 , and 2.5 bar. Table 4 tabulates the various statistical metrics ( $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ , MAE and RMSE) obtained by the 10-fold CV approach and by averaging the results of 100 ML predictions obtained using different randomly chosen training sets of size 10,000 MOFs. Also, Figure 4 shows the variation of the $R^{2}$ and MAE as a function of the training set size for the three pressures. It should be mentioned that the ML predictions reported here seem to be more accurate than those reported in $^{21-24}$ but, since different datasets and evaluation protocols were used in the previous studies, no definite conclusions can be made. Similar to methane, the lower the pressure, the lower the accuracy of the ML algorithm is. However, at any pressure, the inclusion of chemical descriptors and in particular those that are based on the atom types, significantly improves the accuracy of results.
Figure 3 (right panel) shows the variation of MAE at $P=0.5$ bar as a function of the training set size for the three different sets of predictors. It is apparent, again, that when the atom types are combined with the structural descriptors, a specified accuracy in the results is achieved using significantly smaller training set sizes compared to that needed using the two other sets of descriptors. More specifically, the value of MAE obtained for training set sizes of 5000 MOFs using structural descriptors alone or combined with the building blocks can be reached by the structural and atom type based descriptors by using approximately 500 MOFs and 1700 MOFs, respectively. Therefore, less
MOFs by a factor $\sim10$ and $\sim3$ , respectively, are required by the present approach.
## Transferability of the Methodology
The previous results clearly show in terms of ML predictive accuracy, the advantages of using the atom types for the description of the chemical character of MOFs. However, another advantage is that the latter approach is more general when compared to the approach that uses the building blocks that a nanostructure is composed from. In order to validate this point we have used the database of covalent organic frameworks (COFs) created by Mercado et al. 25, along with the structural features and the methane adsorptions capacities at $P=65$ bar of these nanomaterials, which were also provided by the same researchers.
We first created a predictive model using all data available for COFs (in total $N=69,839$ structures). For the training of the ML algorithm a procedure similar with the one described previously for MOFs was used, namely, 10,000 structures were used for the training of the RF algorithm while the remaining data were used for its evaluation. The final results were computed as the average of the results of 100 randomly selected training sets. The $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ statistical metric of the calculations are tabulated in the first two rows of Table 5, together with the corresponding results, previously reported for MOfs (see Table 3). As before, two different sets of descriptors were used by the ML algorithm, namely, the structural features only (second column) and the structural features together with the atom types (third column).
It can be easily seen that for both sets of descriptors, the predictive accuracy of the ML algorithm is higher for COFs than in MOFs. In particular, when the structural features are exclusively used by the ML algorithm the differences in $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ are quite significant (0.907 and 0.969 for MOFs and COFs, respectively). This should be expected since, the simpler composition of COFs and in particular, the lack of metallic corners, reduces the contribution of the chemical character of COFs in favor of their structural characteristics. For both MOFs and COFs, the inclusion of atom types in the set of the ML descriptors improves the accuracy of the ML method. The improvements in $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ are larger for MOFs than in COFs (0.053 for MOFs vs 0.012 for COFs) due to the higher chemical differentiation of MOFs compared to COFs, as explained before, but also due to the already accurate results obtained for COFs using
only the structural features.
In an additional computational experiment, we merged in one the MOFs and COFs datasets and we evaluated the accuracy of the ML model in the obtained MOFs $^+$ COFs dataset using the same protocol described before. The $\mathrm{R^{2}}$ for the two sets of descriptors is provided in the third row of Table 5. It is not surprising the fact that the accuracy of the
