results is in between the accuracy of the ML results obtained for the MOFs and COFs separately. The improvement in $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ by the inclusion of the atom types in the set of descriptors is now 0.032. It should be noted that by using the building blocks instead of the atom types the ML model could not account for the last improvements, since, in general, the building blocks that comprise the MOFs and COFs frameworks are different.
In a final experiment, all structures of MOFs (N=30,439) were used for the construction of an ML predictive model. The model was then used for the prediction of the methane adsorption capacities of COFs. The results ( $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ ) for the two sets of descriptors are tabulated in the last row of Table 5. By examining first the results obtained using only the structural descriptors, it seems unexpected the very low value of $\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.581$ , compared to the $\mathrm{R}^{2}>0.9$ computed in the three previous cases. In order to clarify this point we constructed and compared the distributions of the five geometrical descriptors for the MOFs and COFs (not shown). For almost all descriptors the distributions obtained for MOFs and COFs significantly differ. In order to demonstrate that, instead of providing the distributions itself, we found sufficient to provide the minimum, maximum and mean value of each descriptor for the MOFs and COFs separately (Table 6). It can be seen that the range of values significantly differs. For example, the mass density extends up to $4.24\:\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ for MOFs and up to $\mathrm{1.19g/cm^{3}}$ for COFs, while the average value is $\sim4$ times larger in MOFs. On the other hand, based on Table 6, the surface area, and the dominant and maximum pore diameters are significantly larger in COFs. It is expected that any ML algorithm will perform poorly for cases that significantly differ from those used for its training. As a conclusion from the previous discussion, despite the large number of MOFs (N=30,439) used for the training of the ML method, the dataset was not representative for the construction of an accurate ML model for different nanoporous materials, such as COFs. Although, this is an extreme example of application of the ML algorithm, it is worth mentioning that the inclusion of the atom types significantly improves the accuracy of the predictions for COFs. Table 5 shows that the $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ for COFs obtained using COFs (second row) and MOFs (last row) for the training of the algorithm differ only by $\sim0.1$ ( $\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.981$ and 0.878, respectively) when atom types are included in the descriptors. In contrast, the corresponding results in case that only structural descriptors are used differ by $\sim0.4$ ( $\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.969$ vs $\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.581$ ).
The previous results clearly show, that even though a not representative dataset was used for the construction of a ML model for predictions in COFs, the use of atom types as descriptors that include the chemical character of the nanostructures, finally led to satisfactory predictions.
## Conclusions
In the present work we introduce a universal machine learning algorithm based on fundamental chemical properties. It was shown that the introduction of the “atom types” as descriptors in the ML algorithm in order to account for the chemical character of the material, leads to significant improvements in the ML predictions, with respect to the cases where the chemical character is not considered at all, or the building blocks of the material are used to account for it. For both systems considered (methane and carbon dioxide) and for all pressures examined, significant improvements in the predictions were achieved. It was also seen that training set sizes smaller by a factor of 3-10 are needed in order for the present approach to achieve the accuracy of the previous ones. A second important advantage of the new approach is that with respect to the building blocks, it can be more easily adapted for the study of different materials, as it was found in this study by predicting the properties of the COFs nanomaterials. The last point clearly demonstrates the universality and transferability of our model.
